The cosmic microwave background (CMB) is observed as a nearly isotropic electromagnetic radiation field with the following properties: (i) a temperature of approximately T ≈ 2.725 K; (ii) a spectral energy distribution that is, to extremely high precision, that of a Planckian blackbody at temperature T; (iii) angular isotropy at the level of one part in 10^5 (after subtraction of the dipole); and (iv) a high degree of thermalization across the observable sky. In the standard hot Big Bang scenario, this radiation is interpreted as a relic of a hot, dense, opaque early universe that became transparent at the epoch of recombination (redshift z ≈ 1100), with thermalization achieved while photons and matter were tightly coupled.

An additional empirical constraint is that the universe is observed to be transparent to electromagnetic radiation (including radio frequencies) emitted by sources at high redshift, such as distant radio galaxies and quasars; any proposed thermalization mechanism for the CMB must be compatible with this observed transparency.

Problem. Construct, or prove the impossibility of, an alternative (non–hot Big Bang) physical scenario — for example an astrophysical mechanism (such as thermalization by intergalactic dust, plasma, or stellar relics) or a static / non-expanding cosmological model — that simultaneously:
(a) predicts a background radiation field of temperature T ≈ 2.725 K of the correct energy density;
(b) produces a spectrum that matches a Planckian blackbody to the precision currently measured;
(c) reproduces the observed large-scale isotropy;
(d) provides a quantitatively viable thermalization mechanism yielding (b) and (c); and
(e) does not require the line of sight at the relevant frequencies to be optically thick out to redshifts at which radio galaxies and other compact sources are directly observed.

Either exhibit such a model with quantitative agreement with the above constraints, or prove that conditions (a)–(e) are mutually inconsistent under stated physical assumptions.

